<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cai Yuanpei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1868">1868</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ts'ai Yuan-p'ei</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cai Yuanpei was a Chinese philosopher and politician, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaoxing">Shaoxing</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhejiang">Zhejiang</a>, who was an influential figure in the history of modern Chinese education. A founder of the Guangfuhui in 1904 and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongmenghui">Tongmenghui</a>, he studied at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> and became Minister of Education of the new Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> in 1912. As president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peking-university">Peking University</a> from 1917 he recruited Chen Duxiu, Li Dazhao and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a> as well as conservative scholars, defended academic freedom and admitted the first women in 1920; in 1919 he resigned in protest when the May Fourth student leaders were jailed. In April 1928 he helped found the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/academia-sinica">Academia Sinica</a> and became its first president. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> in 1940.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cai-yuanpei.jpg" alt="Cai Yuanpei" width="330" height="395" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Library of Congress · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cai_Yuanpei-loc-nodate-crop.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Cai Yuanpei</b> resigned as principal of Peking University in protest.</blockquote><a href="/movement/may-fourth-movement">May Fourth Movement · Course of Events</a></li></ul></section>
